<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-sign-in-an-ordered-field" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Sign in an ordered field
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    sign
  </idx>
  
  <idx>
    positive
  </idx>
  
  <idx>
    negative
  </idx>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> \of{ F , + , \cdot , \leq } </m> be an ordered field. As in the case of the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> and the real numbers <m> \R </m>, we can define the <term> sign </term> of an element of <m> F </m> by comparison with the additive identity element <m> 0 </m>:
      
      <ul>
        <li>
          <title>
            Positivity
          </title>
          
          <p>
            A non-zero element <m> x \in F \setminus \set{ 0 } </m> is called <term> positive </term> if <m> 0 \leq x </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Negativity
          </title>
          
          <p>
            A non-zero element <m> x \in F \setminus \set{ 0 } </m> is called <term> negative </term> if <m> x \leq 0 </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Zero
          </title>
          
          <p>
            The additive identity element <m> 0 \in F </m> is not positive or negative.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> x \in F </m>. Show that if <m> x \neq 0 </m>, then <m> x </m> and its additive inverse <m> -x </m> have opposite sign.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        Add <m> -x </m> to the inequality defining the sign of <m> x </m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We first note that if <m> -x = 0 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          x = x + 0 = x + \of{ -x } = 0
        </md>.
        
        Thus if <m> x \neq 0 </m>, then <m> -x \neq 0 </m>. If <m> x </m> is positive, then <m> 0 \leq x </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          -x = 0 + \of{ -x } \leq x + \of{ -x } = 0
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> -x </m> is negative. On the other hand, if <m> x </m> is negative, then <m> x \leq 0 </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          0 = x + \of{ -x } \leq 0 + \of{ -x } = -x
        </md>,
        and so <m> -x </m> is positive.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        More generally, show that for all elements <m> x , y \in F </m>, if <m> x \leq y </m> then <m> -y \leq -x </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            -y \amp = 0 + \of{ -y } = x + \of{ -x } + \of{ -y }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq y + \of{ -x } + \of{ -y } = y + \of{ -y } + \of{ -x } = 0 + \of{ -x } = -x
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that <m> \of{ -1 } \cdot x = -x </m> for all elements <m> x \in F </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          x + \of{ \of{ -1 } \cdot x } = \of{ 1 \cdot x } + \of{ \of{ -1 } \cdot x } = \of{ 1 + \of{ -1 } } \cdot x = 0 \cdot x = 0
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \of{ -1 } \cdot x </m> is an additive inverse for <m> x </m>. Thus (c) in <xref ref="problem-uniqueness-in-a-field"/> implies that <m> -1 \cdot x = -x </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the product of two negative elements is positive.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> x , y \in F </m> be negative elements. Then <m> x \neq 0 </m> and <m> y \neq 0 </m>; we first argue that <m> x \cdot y \neq 0 </m>. Indeed, if <m> x \cdot y = 0 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \amp = 1 \cdot 1 = x \cdot x^{ -1 } \cdot y \cdot y^{ -1 } = x \cdot x^{ -1 } \cdot y \cdot y^{ -1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = x \cdot y \cdot x^{ -1 } \cdot y^{ -1 } = 0 \cdot x^{ -1 } \cdot y^{ -1 } = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        This contradiction implies that <m> x \cdot y \neq 0 </m>. We now observe that (a) implies that <m> -x </m> and <m> -y </m> are positive. Moreover, (c) implies that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x \cdot y \amp = 1 \cdot x \cdot y = \of{ -1 } \cdot \of{ -1 } \cdot x \cdot y
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ -1 } \cdot x \cdot \of{ -1 } \cdot y = \of{ -x } \cdot \of{ -y } \geq 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        is positive.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
